Isolated modules: isolation = "process"
=======================================

Phase 7 of doc/proposals/006-comunicaciones-unificadas.md.  A module can
be run in a process of its own, so that a fault in it is a dead host and
not a dead network.

  Module { name = "history";    isolation = "native";  };   # as always
  Module { name = "partner_api"; isolation = "process"; };  # over there

"native" is the default and is what every module did before this existed.


WHAT IT BUYS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
==================================

A native module shares the server's address space and can write anywhere
in it.  There is no barrier to put inside a process, and pretending
otherwise would be the worst outcome available -- so the barrier is a
process.

What that buys:

  a module that corrupts its heap      takes its host, not the server
  a module that dereferences NULL      takes its host, not the server
  a module that loops for ever         burns one core; the server keeps
                                       serving, and the host is dropped
  a module that leaks                  hits its own rlimit
  a module that stops answering        is dropped, and registrations
                                       start working again

What it does not buy: an isolated module is not *harmless*.  It speaks
the protocol in include/modhost.h and everything it asks for it still
gets -- it can register commands, read features, look clients up and send
them lines.  What it cannot do is reach past the protocol.

**`isolation` is about failure, not about privilege.**  Do not read it as
a sandbox for code you would not otherwise run.

There is a second, quieter thing it buys: an isolated module can be
written in a way that would be unacceptable inside the server.  It may
block, it may take a second over a request, it may call a library that
allocates in ways the core's free lists would not survive.  The server
never waits on it.


WHICH SIDE MAY WAIT
===================

The asymmetry is the whole design.  **The host may block; the server may
not.**

  host -> server      a synchronous request.  The host writes a frame and
                      reads the reply; the server answers from state it
                      already has, without waiting for anything.  That is
                      why a module can call feature_int() or FindUser()
                      over there and get an answer on the spot.
  server -> host      never waited on.  A command is dispatched and
                      forgotten.

A hook that has to be *answered* -- a veto -- is the interesting case,
and it is why HOOK_PENDING had to exist before this could (proposal 006
§5.5).  The server suspends the hook, sends it, and resumes when the
answer comes back.  If no answer comes, FEAT_HOOK_TIMEOUT refuses the
operation, because failing open would be exactly what the veto existed to
prevent.


WHEN A HOST GOES WRONG
======================

  it dies                 the socket closes; the server logs it, reverts
                          everything the module had registered -- its
                          commands, its hooks -- and carries on
  it stops reading        its socket fills; past 256 KiB owed the server
                          gives up on it rather than growing a buffer for
                          a process that is not listening
  it sends nonsense       dropped.  There is no resynchronising a
                          length-prefixed stream, and this is the failure
                          the process boundary exists to contain
  it stops answering      a veto that goes unanswered is refused by
                          FEAT_HOOK_TIMEOUT -- but a module that has
                          stopped answering refuses *every* registration,
                          one deadline at a time, and an operator cannot
                          connect to unload it.  So silence is measured
                          too: a host that was asked something and has
                          said nothing since, for twice FEAT_HOOK_TIMEOUT
                          (never less than 30s), is dropped.
  it will not exit        MODULE UNLOAD closes its socket, waits two
                          seconds and then kills it.  A host that hangs
                          in mi_fini does not get to hold the server.

Every one of those ends with the module unloaded and the server running.
That is the entire claim.


WHAT A MODULE MAY CALL OVER THERE
=================================

Not all of the module API.  A faithful copy of every call would be the
"duplicates the surface of the module API" that proposal 006 §7.7 says the
cost is, so what ircd/modhost/modhost_api.c implements is the profile an
*integration* needs -- which is what isolation is for:

  commands            module_add_command(), module_del_command(),
                      module_command_count()
  hooks               module_add_hook(), module_del_hook(),
                      module_hook_resume()
  the module itself   module_dir(), module_loaded_by()
  the log             log_write()
  memory              MyMalloc(), MyCalloc(), MyFree(), MyRealloc()
  strings             ircd_strncpy(), ircd_strcmp(), ircd_strncmp(),
                      ircd_snprintf(), ircd_vsnprintf()
  features            feature_int(), feature_bool(), feature_str()
  clients             FindUser(), FindClient(), findNUser()
  sending             sendcmdto_one(), sendrawto_one(),
                      sendto_one_numeric()
  the clock           CurrentTime, me

Everything else -- user modes, channel modes, capabilities, bots, the
database, the cache, HTTP routes, workers, migrations, translations,
channels -- is **not** there.

A module that calls one of them does not load isolated, and the failure
is loud: dlopen() is RTLD_NOW, so the host fails at load and the operator
is told which symbol.

  /MODULE LOAD richtext process
  -!- Could not load richtext: .../richtext.so: undefined symbol:
      module_add_cap

That is on purpose.  The dishonest alternative is a stub that silently
does nothing and a module that believes it registered a user mode nobody
has.  Each of the missing calls reaches deep into the server's own state
and is a protocol design of its own rather than a line in a table; the
profile grows when something needs it, and the rule does not change.

**The module is compiled once.**  Nothing in its source says which side
it runs on -- modules/commands/isolated_demo.c is the reference, and it
has no idea.  What decides is the line in ircd.conf.


CLIENTS ARE COPIES
==================

A handler over there is given a real struct Client, so cli_name(),
IsAnOper() and MyConnect() work as its author expects.  It is a **copy**:
it holds what was true when the frame was written, it belongs to the host
process, and it is freed when the call returns.

A module that stores the pointer is storing a pointer to freed memory --
which is exactly the rule a native module already lives under, so there
is nothing new to learn.

What travels back is a numnick, which the server looks up again.  So a
client that left in between is simply not found, rather than a pointer
into freed memory, and a module cannot address anything it was not given.


WHAT THE HOST IS GIVEN, AND WHAT IT IS NOT
==========================================

The host is fork()ed and exec()ed -- not merely forked, because a forked
ircd would carry the whole server's memory and every descriptor it has
open, and the point is that the module has neither.

  it has        one socket (fd 3), the module's path, and rlimits
  it has not    the server's memory, the server's descriptors, a terminal,
                a core file, or the server's process group

The rlimits are the cheap half of proposal 006 §7.7, and they are on from
the first version: 512 MiB of address space, 3600 seconds of CPU, 64
files, no core.  They protect nothing of the server's -- nothing inside a
process does -- but a leak, a runaway loop or a descriptor flood ends at
the host, and a host that the kernel stops is a host the server notices
going.


OPERATING IT
============

  /MODULE LIST                        says "isolated" or "in the server"
  /MODULE LOAD <name> process         load it into a host
  /MODULE LOAD <name> native          load it into the server (default)
  /MODULE RELOAD <name>               keeps whichever it had

A rehash that changes a module's `isolation` unloads and loads it, in
that order: the old code stops before the new copy starts, whichever
direction it is going.  A rehash that does not change it leaves the host
alone.

ircu-modhost is installed beside the ircd (IRCU_HOSTPATH, defaulting to
the directory SPATH is in) and the two are one version: the handshake
compares their ABI and refuses a mismatch, so they are upgraded together.
Inside a chroot the host has to be below the new root, like every other
path compiled into the server.


WHEN TO USE IT
==============

Use "process" for what you did not write and do not review: integrations,
third-party modules, anything that runs a client's logic.

Use "native" for your own code on the critical path -- history, the
file host, the database driver -- both because those need the parts of
the API that are not in the profile and because an extra process between
the server and its own database buys nothing.

The rule worth keeping: **no third-party module goes in native.**


